# Coin Toss — environment variables.
#
# Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and uncomment what you need. Every value below is
# shown at its built-in default, so an empty `.env` behaves exactly like no
# `.env` at all.
#
# Two groups, and the difference matters:
#
#   VITE_*  Read by Vite. Client-facing ones are INLINED INTO THE BUNDLE at
#           build time — they are public, readable by anyone who opens
#           devtools. Never put a secret here. Changing one requires a rebuild.
#
#   MOCK_*  Read by the mock aggregator (`server/index.js`), which runs as its
#           own Node process with no dotenv. These must come from the real
#           shell/CI environment — a `.env` file will NOT reach them.
#           e.g.  MOCK_FORCE_OUTCOME=side npm run dev
#
# Several defaults also differ between `npm run dev` and a production build,
# via Vite's built-in `import.meta.env.DEV`. That flag is set by the build
# itself and is not something you set here.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client — baked into the bundle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which of the two betting-client integrations this build serves. The same game
# ships to partners and to aggregators over genuinely different APIs — different
# launch params, different endpoints, a one-call round versus a four-call one.
# Each has its contract in docs/.
#
#   (unset, the DEFAULT) -> partner
#   aggregator           -> aggregator
#
# Only the exact string "aggregator" selects the aggregator; anything else,
# including a typo, resolves to partner rather than to a broken third state.
#
# It is a BUILD-TIME switch: the unselected integration is tree-shaken out, so
# changing this needs a rebuild, not just a reload. Note the two contracts also
# read different launch params — partner requires `?clientId=<slug>-<AES_HEX>`
# and reads `?lang=`, aggregator needs no params and reads `?language=`.
# VITE_INTEGRATION=aggregator

# Sub-path the game is mounted under when served through a proxy — e.g.
# `/games/cointoss` for https://host/games/cointoss/.
#
# Sets Vite's `base` (hashed JS/CSS) and is applied at runtime by
# `src/assetUrl.ts` to everything in `public/`, which Vite copies verbatim and
# never rewrites: sprites, the click sound, the boot logo, the Bestime font,
# and the whole preload manifest.
#
# WITHOUT a trailing slash — both Vite and `assetUrl()` add the separator, so
# "/games/cointoss/" produces a doubled one.
#
# Unset (the default) builds with a relative base and relative asset URLs,
# which resolve correctly at any depth for a page served as `index.html`. That
# is the right setting for a plain root deploy, Vercel included. Set it only
# when a proxy rewrites the URL path without also rewriting the document path.
# VITE_BASE_PATH=/games/cointoss

# Origin serving the localization API (`/lang/api/v1/languages`), no trailing
# slash. See `src/i18n/languageClient.ts`.
#
# Default in dev: empty — same-origin, which the proxy below forwards to the
# mock server, so translations work fully offline.
# Default in a build: https://game.shacksevo.co (a real cross-origin GET; that
# host returns Access-Control-Allow-Origin for the deploy origin).
#
# A failed fetch is survivable — `LanguageContext.boot` falls back to the
# built-in English strings — so a wrong value here degrades quietly rather
# than breaking the launch.
# VITE_LANGUAGE_BASE=https://game.shacksevo.co

# Aggregator host + API base for the token exchange. Host and API base ONLY —
# `src/api/client.ts` appends the `/partner/agg/token` path itself, so give it
# e.g. `https://staging.example.com/user/api/v2`, not the full endpoint URL. A
# trailing slash is trimmed.
#
# Default when unset: https://game.shacksevo.co/user/api/v2
# (so the endpoint is https://game.shacksevo.co/user/api/v2/partner/agg/token)
#
# Full resolution order in `resolveTokenUrl()`, highest first:
#   1. `?mock=1` on a built deploy -> <origin>/api/mock/api/v2/partner/agg/token
#      The bundled serverless mock wins over this variable, so a preview never
#      depends on a configured host or on aggregator credentials. A deliberate
#      no-op in dev, where /api/** is already proxied to the mock.
#   2. this variable, when set
#   3. the default above
#
# NOTE there is no dev branch: `npm run dev` does NOT target the local mock
# under the aggregator integration. Set this to `http://localhost:5175/api/v2`
# to play against it, which the Vite proxy forwards to server/index.js.
#
# Set this to point a build at a staging aggregator. It is a URL, not a
# credential — the launch token itself arrives as a query parameter.
# VITE_AGGREGATOR_TOKEN_HOST=https://game.shacksevo.co/user/api/v2


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dev server — `vite.config.ts` only, never shipped to the client
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Port for the Vite dev client. `strictPort` is on, so a busy port fails loudly
# instead of silently sliding to the next one. Override to run this replica
# alongside its sibling projects.
# VITE_PORT=5175

# Where the dev proxy forwards `/api` and `/lang`. Defaults to
# http://localhost:$MOCK_PORT (8787). Point it at a real backend to develop
# the UI against live data instead of the mock.
# VITE_MOCK_SERVER=http://localhost:8787


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mock aggregator — shell/CI only, NOT read from this file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `server/index.js` is a fake wallet for local development and preview
# deploys. It has no dotenv, so set these on the command line.

# Port the mock listens on. MUST stay in sync with the dev proxy above — when
# these drifted apart, every /api and /lang call hit a dead port and the game
# showed its "HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request" fatal-error screen. Deliberately not
# read from `.env`: that would move the proxy target without moving the
# listener, recreating exactly that bug.
# MOCK_PORT=8787

# Force every round's outcome instead of rolling. One of: head | tail | side.
# Anything else (including unset) uses the real distribution — head 48% /
# tail 48% / side 4%. Handy for exercising the always-losing `side` landing.
# MOCK_FORCE_OUTCOME=side

# Accept any token matching the shape `mintToken()` produces (32 lowercase hex)
# rather than checking it against an in-memory set. Set to "1".
# `api/mock.js` turns this on for itself: serverless invocations share no
# memory, so a token minted by one instance is unknown to the next. Safe only
# because this backend is a fake wallet with nothing to protect — never enable
# it against anything real.
# MOCK_STATELESS_TOKENS=1

# Set to "1" to have the first `agg-authenticate` report an abandoned open
# round, so the client's settle-on-load reconciliation path runs end to end.
# MOCK_OPEN_ROUND=1
